<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      Runs the IDE#### code-style analyzers as part of the build instead of only in an editor, so
      CI sees them. Which of them are on is decided in .editorconfig; today that is IDE0005
      (unnecessary using directive) and nothing else.
    -->
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      WARNINGS ARE ERRORS IN CI, AND ONLY IN CI.

      The build is clean (0 warnings), and this is what keeps it clean: a new warning fails the
      build on the server. Locally it stays a warning, because half-finished work with an unused
      variable in it is a normal state to be in and a gate that punishes experimentation gets
      switched off by the people it is meant to help.

      `CI` is set to `true` by GitHub Actions, and by Azure Pipelines, GitLab and Travis. MSBuild
      reads environment variables as properties, so nothing has to be passed on the command line.
      To reproduce a CI failure locally:
        CI=true dotnet build InfoCarrier.Core.slnx -c Release

      THE CONFIGURATION IS PART OF THE COMMAND. Omitting it builds Debug, where the Blazor sample
      does not run the trim analyzer, so the whole class of diagnostic that fails the server is
      invisible. That omission kept the CI build red from M8-32 to N12 while a local `CI=true`
      build reported `0 Warning(s), 0 Error(s)` every time.

      `-c Release` rather than the long spelling, and that is not a style choice: a double hyphen
      cannot appear inside an XML comment. Writing the long form here made this file unparseable,
      which MSBuild reported as `NETSDK1124: Trimming assemblies requires .NET Core 3.0 or higher`
      — because with the file unreadable there was no TargetFramework to satisfy the check. The
      error names trimming and has nothing to do with it.

      There is deliberately no `WarningsNotAsErrors`. EF1001 was the one code that needed an
      exemption, and since M8-30 it needs none: the 19 files that use EF Core internals carry a
      file-scoped `#pragma warning disable EF1001`, which is what EF Core's own providers do, so
      the build emits none. A NEW file reaching for an internal API still warns, and in CI now
      fails, which is the tripwire, working as intended. Do not add a NoWarn to silence it.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      ILLink's own warnings are never errors. The trimmed Blazor publish reports 88 IL2xxx
      warnings owned by InfoCarrier.Core, unfixable by design: the wire carries a type's NAME, so
      Assembly.GetType(string) and MakeGenericMethod are what this provider is made of, and
      [DynamicallyAccessedMembers] cannot describe them. eng/trim-ratchet.sh gates their
      DIRECTION against eng/trim-baseline.txt.

      This property covers the ILLink TASK only. It is NOT what keeps the trim ratchet green, and
      believing it was cost a run: most IL2xxx findings come from the trim ROSLYN ANALYZER during
      compilation (they carry a source line), so plain TreatWarningsAsErrors turns those into
      errors and this property never sees them. eng/trim-ratchet.sh therefore passes
      -p:TreatWarningsAsErrors=false on its own publish, and says why.
    -->
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Company>InfoCarrier.Core</Company>
    <Product>InfoCarrier.Core</Product>
    <Description>Entity Framework Core database provider that remotes LINQ queries and change-tracking over a wire protocol.</Description>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Package identity. Applies to every project, which is why IsPackable is FALSE here: `dotnet
    pack` on the solution would otherwise produce a package for each sample and each test project.
    The two products under src/ opt in individually.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Package identity">
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      THE VERSION IS THE GIT TAG. There is no version number in this file, and that is the point:
      it used to be here *and* in the tag, and `release.yml` carried a step whose only job was to
      check that the two agreed. That step was broken — a stale `grep -v Abstractions` left over
      from M8-22, so the glob also matched InfoCarrier.Core.AspNetCore and every tagged release
      would have failed on it. MinVer removes the disagreement instead of policing it.

      To release:  git tag -a v10.0.0-preview.2 -m "..."  &&  git push origin v10.0.0-preview.2
      To see what a build will produce:  dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Version

      An untagged commit builds as the next patch with a height — `10.0.1-alpha.0.7` — which is
      unique, ordered, and exactly what an internal CI feed wants.

      `MinVerMinimumMajorMinor` is 10.0 because the major.minor tracks Entity Framework Core's,
      which is the convention every EF provider follows and the fastest way for a reader to know
      which EF a package targets. It also keeps a repository with no release tag yet inside the
      10.0 line rather than starting at 0.0.

      `-preview` stays until the public surface is settled. A stable 10.0.0 is a promise not to
      break it, and that promise is not ready to be made.

      Do NOT restate that reason in a user-facing document. It used to say "until a gRPC binding
      and streaming results are settled", and that sentence propagated into the README, the package
      readme, the site index, the installation page, the limitations page and the GitHub release
      body as a roadmap promise. Both features went out of scope for v10 on 2026-08-23 (roadmap,
      M8 exit criteria) and every copy had to be hunted down. The version number carries the
      information on its own, which is what EF Core relies on. See docs/doc-style.md rule 6.

      MinVer needs the tags, so any workflow that builds must check out with `fetch-depth: 0`.
      A shallow clone has no tags, and the version silently becomes a default.
    -->
    <MinVerTagPrefix>v</MinVerTagPrefix>
    <MinVerMinimumMajorMinor>10.0</MinVerMinimumMajorMinor>

    <!--
      Pinned, and NOT derived from the package version. An AssemblyVersion that moves with every
      patch forces every consumer to rebuild or to carry a binding redirect, for a change that is
      by definition compatible. Major only, which is what EF Core and ASP.NET Core both do.
      FileVersion and InformationalVersion still carry the full version, and the latter carries the
      commit SHA — `10.0.0-preview.1+901bc81…` — so a binary is still traceable to a source line.
    -->
    <AssemblyVersion>10.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>

    <Authors>Alexander Zabluda</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) Alexander Zabluda</Copyright>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/azabluda/InfoCarrier.Core</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/azabluda/InfoCarrier.Core.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageTags>entity-framework-core;efcore;ef-core-provider;linq;n-tier;multi-tier;blazor;wpf;expression-trees;change-tracking</PackageTags>

    <!--
      The package README is docs/nuget-readme.md rather than the repository README, and the reason
      is mechanical: nuget.org renders a README but resolves images only against ABSOLUTE URLs, so
      the banner the repository README opens with would show as a broken image. The repository
      README also links to a dozen relative docs paths that mean nothing inside a package.
    -->
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

    <!--
      EMBEDDED, not PackageIconUrl. The old URL form is deprecated and renders nothing on
      nuget.org for new packages; an embedded icon travels inside the .nupkg and works offline and
      in Visual Studio's package manager. Each packable project adds the file itself, next to the
      README, because both are pulled in from outside the project directory.

      128x128, which is what nuget.org recommends. It is displayed far smaller than that in list
      views (32px in Visual Studio), which is why the mark is a shape and three glyphs rather
      than a wordmark: the "InfoCarrier.Core" text in the original draft is illegible below about
      64px and is dropped.
    -->
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>

    <!-- A separate .snupkg, which is what nuget.org's symbol server accepts. -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>

    <!-- SourceLink: step into this package's source from a debugger. Built into the SDK. -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Signing">
    <SignAssembly>false</SignAssembly>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every project, not only the two packable ones, so that a test or sample assembly reports the
    same version as the product it exercises. `PrivateAssets="all"` keeps it out of the nuspec: it
    is a build-time task, and nothing InfoCarrier ships depends on it at run time.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MinVer" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
